this is the latest crackdown on press  freedom in hong kong  police arresting the acting chief editor  of pro-democracy stand news  an eyewitness filming patrick lam being  removed from its offices  police attempted to keep people from  filming their raid involving hundreds of  officers  but later confirmed at least six people  had been arrested  hong kong police chief told a news  conference they were detained for  conspiring to publish inflammatory  articles i think the the the primary  concern or the the the number one  concern for us is don't be biased  don't be biased  you you know well how to report how to  be a responsible  reporter how to  how to make a non-biased report to your  readers i know they're talking to  reporter we are not talking the  the media we just target the national  security  law the offenses  if you just won't report i don't think  this is a problem  as well as the arrests of senior staff  at the media outlet former board member  and senior barrister margaret ing was  also detained according to local media  as well as pop star and prominent  activist denise ho  her arrest announced on her social media  account  a subsequent message posted on her  behalf urged friends and supporters not  to worry about her  shortly after the raid on its offices  stand news announced its closure online  saying it had halted operations and all  staff were immediately dismissed  stand news was the most prominent  pro-democracy publication left in hong  kong since the closure of the tabloid  apple daily earlier this year  police charged its former publisher with  sedition on tuesday  sedition is not a crime under hong  kong's national security law imposed by  beijing last year  but this latest raid is adding to  concern the legislation is being used to  stifle freedom of speech anybody who  attempts  to make use of  media work  as a tool  to pursue  their political purpose  or other interests  contravenes the law  particularly  offenses that endanger national security  they are  the evil elements  that  damage press freedom  stand news had already halted  subscriptions and removed opinion pieces  from its website earlier this year now  its raid enclosure adds to growing fears  over press freedoms in hong kong  sally lockwood sky news
 

